\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\taxon{Remark}
\title{Token generation as a special case}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{agent}
\tag{language-model}

\p{Autoregressive generation fits the preceding setup. The action space is the
vocabulary, the public history is the token prefix, and the observation after
an action can be taken to be that emitted token. An end token or length limit
supplies the termination rule.}

\p{Agentic interaction adds an environment step between some model decisions.
The compact diagram records the change of interface. It does not identify a
token with a tool call or assume that every observation is text.}

\tikzfig{\begin{tikzpicture}[
  box/.style={draw, rounded corners=2pt, align=center, font=\small,
    text width=35mm, minimum height=10mm},
  flow/.style={-{Stealth[length=2mm]}, thick}
]
\node[box] (hist) at (0,1.4) {public history $H_t$};
\node[box] (pol) at (5.2,1.4) {policy $\pi_t(\cdot\mid H_t)$};
\node[box] (act) at (5.2,-1.0) {token or tool action $A_t$};
\node[box] (env) at (0,-1.0) {environment observation $O_{t+1}$};
\draw[flow] (hist) -- (pol);
\draw[flow] (pol) -- (act);
\draw[flow] (act) -- (env);
\draw[flow] (env) -- (hist);
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{The first agentic theorem questions concern this interface. When does a
token policy induce a well-defined utterance or tool policy? How does an error
probability compound with the number of required environment transitions?
Which parts of a terminal observation can be used for later training without
violating non-anticipation?}
